.TH MAKENSIS 1 2026-10-20 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
makensis \- compile NSIS scripts into Windows installers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B makensis
.RI [ option " | " script " |"
.BR \- "] ..."
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B makensis
compiles scripts for NSIS 3.13, the Nullsoft Scriptable Install System,
into a Windows installer: a stub program followed by the compressed
files, settings, and code that the scripts describe.
Scripts are conventionally named
.IR *.nsi ,
and the headers they include
.IR *.nsh .
Progress messages and warnings go to standard output, and errors to
standard error.
.PP
The arguments are processed in order, so an option affects only the
scripts after it:
.B makensis /DX a.nsi
defines
.B X
for
.IR a.nsi ,
but
.B makensis a.nsi /DX
does not.
Several scripts are treated as one concatenated script that produces a
single installer.
A
.I script
that cannot be opened is tried again with
.B .nsi
appended.
A
.I script
named
.B \-
is read from standard input.
.PP
Before reading each script file,
.B makensis
changes to its directory, so that relative paths in the script are
relative to the script.
The change remains in effect, so a relative
.I script
path later on the command line is relative to the previous script.
.PP
The installer is written to the path named by the script's
.B OutFile
command, replacing any file there.
A relative path is taken from the current directory when the installer
is written, which is normally the directory of the last script.
Without
.BR OutFile ,
the path is the full path of the first script file up to its last
period, if any, followed by
.BR .exe .
That period may be in a directory name.
A script read from standard input gives no default.
.PP
Before the first script,
.B makensis
reads two files of script code common to every installer, each only if
it exists:
.I nsisconf.nsh
in the directory named by
.BR NSISCONFDIR ,
or else in the parent of the compiler's directory,
.IR share/nsis ,
even when
.B NSISDIR
is set, and then
.IR %APPDATA%\ensisconf.nsh .
.B /NOCONFIG
skips both.
.SS Scripts
A script is a sequence of commands, one per line.
A line ending in a backslash continues on the next, and
.B ;
and
.B #
begin comments, as does
.B /*
up to
.BR */ .
Attributes such as
.BR Name ,
.BR OutFile ,
and
.B RequestExecutionLevel
configure the installer,
.B Section
and
.B Function
blocks hold the instructions it runs, and
.B Page
commands choose its pages.
Commands beginning with
.BR ! ,
such as
.BR !include ,
.BR !define ,
.BR !ifdef ,
.BR !macro ,
and
.BR !system ,
are preprocessor commands, run at compile time.
In arguments,
.BI ${ name }
expands a symbol from
.B !define
or
.BR /D ,
.BI $% name %
an environment variable at compile time, and
.BI $ name
a variable when the installer runs.
.B /CMDHELP
prints the syntax of every command.
.SS Data directory
.B makensis
finds its stubs, plugins, and headers in its data directory: the parent
of the directory that holds the compiler, which is
.I share/nsis
in the kit, or
.B NSISDIR
when set.
Scripts see it as
.BR ${NSISDIR} .
It holds:
.TP
.I Stubs
The installer stubs, one for each compressor, solid or not, and target,
named like
.IR lzma_solid\-amd64\-unicode .
The compressors are
.BR zlib ,
the default,
.BR bzip2 ,
and
.BR lzma ,
chosen with
.BR SetCompressor .
Also
.IR uninst ,
the default uninstaller icon.
.TP
.IR Plugins/amd64\-unicode ", " Plugins/x86\-unicode
The plugin DLLs of the NSIS distribution, such as
.BR System ,
.BR nsDialogs ,
and
.BR nsExec ,
for each target.
A script calls their functions as
\fIplugin\fB::\fIfunction\fR.
.TP
.I Include
The headers, such as
.I MUI2.nsh
for the Modern UI and
.I LogicLib.nsh
for structured control flow.
.B !include
searches the current directory, then this directory, then those added by
.BR !addincludedir .
.TP
.I Contrib
Files the headers use: Modern UI pages, graphics, language files, and
alternative user interface resources.
.TP
.I Bin
The compiler,
.IR makensis.exe ,
and
.I RegTool\-amd64.bin
.RI ( RegTool\-x86.bin
in the x86 kit), which
.I Library.nsh
embeds to register DLLs after a reboot.
.SS Targets
The target is the processor and character set of the installer:
.B amd64\-unicode
or
.BR x86\-unicode .
The x64 kit has stubs and plugins for both and defaults to
.BR amd64\-unicode .
The x86 kit has only
.BR x86\-unicode .
A script selects a target with a command such as
.BR "Target x86\-unicode" ,
before any file is compressed.
.B makensis
also knows
.B x86\-ansi
and
.BR arm64\-unicode ,
but the kit has no stubs for them, so selecting them, including with
.BR "Unicode false" ,
fails with an error ending in
.BR "(adequate stub not found?)" .
.PP
.B CPU amd64
and
.B CPU x86
also select a target, but
.B CPU x86
chooses between
.B x86\-ansi
and
.B x86\-unicode
by a flag that is uninitialized unless an earlier command changed the
target from an x86 one.
It may therefore select
.B x86\-ansi
and fail, as it does in the x64 kit.
Use
.B Target
instead.
.SS Predefined symbols
.B /HDRINFO
lists the symbols defined before any script, most of which describe how
.B makensis
was built.
These are the most useful:
.TP
.B NSISDIR
The data directory.
.TP
.B NSIS_VERSION
The version, such as
.BR v19\-Oct\-2026.cvs ;
see
.BR NOTES .
.TP
.B NSIS_PACKEDVERSION
The version as a number,
.BR 0x03012666 .
.TP
.B NSIS_CPU
.B amd64
or
.BR x86 ,
from the target.
.TP
.B NSIS_PTR_SIZE
The size of a pointer in the installer,
.B 8
or
.BR 4 .
.TP
.B NSIS_CHAR_SIZE
.BR 2 ,
the size of a character in the installer.
.TP
.BR NSIS_AMD64 ", " NSIS_IX86
.B NSIS_AMD64
is
.B 1
for
.BR amd64\-unicode ,
and
.B NSIS_IX86
is
.B 400
for
.BR x86\-unicode .
Only one is defined.
.TP
.B NSIS_UNICODE
Defined, since every available target is Unicode.
.TP
.BR NSIS_WIN32_MAKENSIS ", " NSIS_UNICODE_MAKENSIS
Defined.
.TP
.B NSIS_MAKENSIS64
Defined in the x64 kit, whose compiler is a 64-bit program.
.PP
The target symbols change when the script changes the target.
Standard predefines such as
.B __FILE__
and
.B __LINE__
are also available.
.SH OPTIONS
Each option begins with
.B /
or
.BR \- ,
so
.B /NOCD
and
.B \-NOCD
are the same, and option names are case-insensitive.
An argument shown attached, as in
.BR /V2 ,
must be attached, and others are the next argument.
After
.BR \-\- ,
every argument is a script, so
.B \-
then names a file, and a script path beginning with
.B /
or
.B \-
can follow.
.PP
.BR /NOTIFYHWND ,
.BR /O ,
.BR /OUTPUTCHARSET ,
.BR /PPO ,
.BR /RAW ,
.BR /SAFEPPO ,
.BR /VERSION ,
and
.B /WX
take effect only before the first script file name, not counting
.BR \- ,
and before
.BR \-\- .
Later, they are ignored, except
.BR /VERSION ,
which is then unrecognized.
An unrecognized option stops the processing of arguments:
.B makensis
prints the usage message and exits with status 1 without writing an
installer.
.TP
\fB/CMDHELP\fR [\fIitem\fR]
Print the syntax of the script command
.IR item ,
or of every command, on standard output.
The next argument, if there is one, is always taken as
.IR item ,
even when it looks like an option or a script.
An unknown
.I item
prints an error, but processing continues.
.TP
.B /HDRINFO
Print the sizes of the installer's header structures and every symbol
defined so far, including the build options of
.BR makensis .
.TP
.B /LICENSE
Print the NSIS license.
.TP
.B /VERSION
Print the version, without a newline, and exit with status 0.
Of the other arguments, only the options listed above that precede it
take effect.
.TP
.B /HELP
Print the usage message on standard output.
.TP
.BI /P n
Set the priority of the process:
.B 0
idle,
.B 1
below normal,
.B 2
normal,
.B 3
above normal,
.B 4
high, or
.B 5
realtime, which also prints a warning.
.TP
.BI /V n
Set the verbosity:
.B 0
prints nothing,
.B 1
only errors,
.B 2
also warnings,
.B 3
also information, the default, and
.B 4
also a line for each script command processed.
Given before the first script,
.B /V3
or
.B /V4
first prints a banner with the version, unless
.B /PPO
or
.B /SAFEPPO
is given.
.BR /CMDHELP ,
.BR /HDRINFO ,
and
.B /LICENSE
print nothing below 3.
.TP
.B /WX
Treat warnings as errors: the first warning ends
.B makensis
with status 1.
.TP
.BI /O file
Write all output, including errors, to
.IR file ,
replacing its contents.
If it cannot be opened,
.B makensis
prints a warning and uses standard output.
.TP
.B /LAUNCH
After writing the installer, run it as if it were opened in Explorer,
without waiting for it.
.TP
.B /PAUSE
Before exiting, print
.B MakeNSIS done \- hit enter to close...\&
and wait for a newline, carriage return, or Escape on standard input,
forever if standard input is at end of file.
A
.B \-
argument after
.B /PAUSE
cancels it.
.TP
.B /NOCONFIG
Do not read the
.I nsisconf.nsh
files.
It must come before the first script.
.TP
.B /NOCD
Do not change to the directory of each script that follows.
.TP
\fB/INPUTCHARSET\fR \fIcharset\fR, \fB/ICS\fR \fIcharset\fR
Read the scripts that follow, when they have no byte order mark, in
.IR charset :
.B ACP
for the ANSI code page, the default;
.B OEM
for the OEM code page;
.BI CP n
for code page
.IR n ;
.BR UTF8 ;
or
.BR UTF16LE .
.B UTF16BE
also selects UTF-16LE.
Names are case-insensitive, and
.B UTF
may be followed by a hyphen or an underscore.
.B AUTO
selects the default, and so does any other name, with a warning.
Files read by
.B !include
are not affected.
.TP
\fB/OUTPUTCHARSET\fR \fIcharset\fR, \fB/OCS\fR \fIcharset\fR
Write output that does not go to a console, including the
.B /O
log, in
.IR charset ,
named as for
.BR /INPUTCHARSET ,
or as
.B UTF8SIG
for UTF-8 with a byte order mark, or
.B UTF16LEBOM
for UTF-16LE with one.
The default is
.BR ACP .
When standard output or standard error is a file that already begins
with a byte order mark, and that
.B makensis
can also read, it uses that file's encoding instead.
The kit's shell opens redirected files for writing only, so this does
not apply there.
An unknown
.IR charset ,
including
.BR AUTO ,
prints the usage message and exits with status 1.
.TP
.B /RAW
Like
.BR "/OUTPUTCHARSET UTF16LE" ,
but never take the encoding from a byte order mark.
.TP
.B /PPO
Preprocess only: write the script to standard output with symbols
substituted, macros expanded, and conditional blocks resolved, then exit
without writing an installer.
Preprocessor commands run as usual, except that
.BR !packhdr ,
.BR !finalize ,
and
.B !uninstfinalize
are written out instead.
The verbosity is at most 1.
.TP
.B /SAFEPPO
Like
.BR /PPO ,
but also write out, instead of running,
.BR !system ,
.BR !execute ,
.BR !makensis ,
.BR !appendfile ,
.BR !delfile ,
and
.BR !tempfile .
.TP
\fB/D\fIname\fR[\fB=\fIvalue\fR]
Define the symbol
.I name
as
.IR value ,
or as empty, for the scripts that follow, like
.BR !define .
A name already defined, such as
.B NSISDIR
or one from an earlier
.BR /D ,
keeps its value.
A later
.B !define
of the same name is an error.
.TP
.BI /X command
Process
.I command
as a line of script, in its place among the arguments.
Messages that give its location name the file
.B <command line>
and, as the line, its position among the arguments plus one.
Commands before the first script run before the
.I nsisconf.nsh
files are read.
Without any script, the
.B /X
commands make up the whole script, and no
.I nsisconf.nsh
is read.
.TP
.BI "/NOTIFYHWND " hwnd
Report to the window whose handle is the decimal number
.IR hwnd ,
through the protocol of the MakeNSISW front end, which the kit does not
include.
It is ignored when
.I hwnd
is not a window.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B APPDATA
The directory of the per-user
.IR nsisconf.nsh ,
read after the main one.
.TP
.B COMSPEC
The command interpreter, normally
.BR cmd.exe ,
that runs the commands of
.BR !system ,
.BR !packhdr ,
.BR !finalize ,
and
.BR !uninstfinalize .
When it is empty,
.B CMD.EXE
is used, and when it is unset, these commands terminate
.B makensis
abnormally.
.TP
.B NSISCONFDIR
The directory of the main
.IR nsisconf.nsh ,
instead of the parent of the compiler's directory.
.TP
.B NSISDIR
The data directory, instead of the parent of the compiler's directory.
It does not move the main
.IR nsisconf.nsh .
.TP
.BR TMP ", " TEMP
The directory for temporary files, as chosen by
.BR GetTempPath .
.PP
Scripts can read any variable at compile time as
.BI $% name %\fR.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success: the installer was written, preprocessing finished, or
.B /VERSION
ran.
Without a script or
.B /X
command,
.BR /HDRINFO ,
.BR /HELP ,
.BR /LICENSE ,
or a valid
.B /CMDHELP
also exits with 0.
.TP
.B 1
An error, such as a script that cannot be opened, an error in a script
or an
.IR nsisconf.nsh ,
a warning under
.BR /WX ,
an unrecognized option, no script, missing stubs, or Ctrl+C.
.TP
.B 382
The launcher could not start the compiler.
It prints a message beginning
.B w64devkit (alias):
on standard error.
The kit's shell sees it as 126, its low byte.
.SH EXAMPLES
Write a script for an installer that copies
.I hello.exe
into a directory the user chooses, without asking for administrator
rights, and compile it into
.IR hello\-setup.exe :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ cat >hello.nsi <<\(aqEOF\(aq
Name "Hello"
OutFile "hello\-setup.exe"
InstallDir "$LOCALAPPDATA\eHello"
RequestExecutionLevel user
Page directory
Page instfiles
Section
  SetOutPath "$INSTDIR"
  File "hello.exe"
SectionEnd
EOF
$ makensis hello.nsi
.EE
.RE
.PP
Build a 32-bit installer with the x64 kit, under another name.
The
.B Target
command must come before the script, and the
.B OutFile
command after it, to override the script's own:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ makensis "/XTarget x86\-unicode" hello.nsi \e
    "/XOutFile hello32\-setup.exe"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print only warnings and errors, and define
.B VERSION
for a script that uses
.BR ${VERSION} :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ makensis /V2 /DVERSION=1.2.3 hello.nsi
.EE
.RE
.PP
Compress with solid LZMA without editing the script:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ makensis "/XSetCompressor /SOLID lzma" hello.nsi
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the syntax of a command:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ makensis /CMDHELP SetCompressor
Usage: SetCompressor [/FINAL] [/SOLID] (zlib|bzip2|lzma)
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show the script after preprocessing, without letting it run programs or
change files:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ makensis /SAFEPPO hello.nsi >hello.txt
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
The kit's
.I bin/makensis.exe
is a launcher that runs
.I share/nsis/Bin/makensis.exe
with the same arguments, waits for it, and exits with its status.
The launcher ignores Ctrl+C, leaving it to the compiler, which then
exits with status 1.
.PP
The kit builds NSIS 3.13 without a release version, so
.B /VERSION
and
.B ${NSIS_VERSION}
give the date of the build, as in
.BR v19\-Oct\-2026.cvs ,
and
.B ${NSIS_PACKEDVERSION}
is
.BR 0x03012666 ,
below the
.B 0x03013000
of a 3.13 release.
A script that requires NSIS 3.13 or later by comparing
.B ${NSIS_PACKEDVERSION}
rejects the kit.
.PP
The kit omits the NSIS documentation and examples,
.IR nsisconf.nsh ,
the MakeNSISW front end, zip2exe, MakeLangId, the VPatch patch generator
GenPat, and the
.B x86\-ansi
and
.B arm64\-unicode
stubs and plugins.
The x64 kit has only
.IR RegTool\-amd64.bin ,
so an
.B x86\-unicode
installer cannot use
.B InstallLib
from
.I Library.nsh
to register a library on reboot, as with the
.B REBOOT_PROTECTED
and
.B REBOOT_NOTPROTECTED
options:
.B makensis
stops with an error that RegTool is missing.
.PP
A script file named on the command line without a byte order mark is
read in the ANSI code page unless
.B /INPUTCHARSET
says otherwise, but the files it includes default to UTF-8.
Save scripts as UTF-8 with a byte order mark, or pass
.BR "/INPUTCHARSET UTF8" .
.PP
Output to a console goes through the Unicode console API, with warnings
in yellow and errors in red.
Other output, including the
.B /O
log, is in the ANSI code page unless
.B /OUTPUTCHARSET
says otherwise, and has CRLF line endings.
.PP
.BR !system ,
.BR !packhdr ,
.BR !finalize ,
and
.B !uninstfinalize
run their commands with
.BR cmd.exe ,
not the kit's
.BR sh .
.B !execute
and
.B !makensis
start programs directly.
.PP
Shells that rewrite arguments beginning with
.B /
as paths, such as those of MSYS2 and Git for Windows, garble the
.B /
forms of the options.
Use the
.B \-
forms there.
